WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.458 --> 00:00:01.876
[audience applause]

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:05.880
These are the 7 most powerful men in China.

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:09.092
They represent the top leadership positions
of the ruling party...

00:00:09.217 --> 00:00:11.720
and their identities are revealed 
every five years...

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:14.139
at a carefully choreographed 
political event

00:00:14.139 --> 00:00:15.807
called the National Congress.

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:18.727
“China's Communist Party unveiled 
its new leadership

00:00:18.727 --> 00:00:20.061
over the next five years.”

00:00:20.562 --> 00:00:22.105
The order of appearance is symbolic.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:23.773
The first person to emerge

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:26.026
is the head of the party 
for the next five years.

00:00:26.526 --> 00:00:29.112
At the most recent event 
in October 2022...

00:00:29.404 --> 00:00:31.698
that person was Xi Jinping

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:32.907
China's president for the last ten years...

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:36.244
who walked out in the top spot 
a historic third time

00:00:36.244 --> 00:00:38.455
after getting rid of China's 
presidential term limit

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:41.458
that restricted all his predecessors 
to two terms.

00:00:41.666 --> 00:00:44.627
A signal that he may be planning 
to stay in power for life.

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:48.048
The world hasn't seen a Chinese leader like this

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.174
since Mao Zedong...

00:00:49.758 --> 00:00:52.427
the revolutionary founder of the 
People's Republic of China...

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.429
whose ruthless dictatorship

00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:56.931
scarred the Chinese people 
for generations.

00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:59.517
Xi has been compared to Mao a lot.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:02.020
And he clearly draws from Mao's playbook.

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.773
But there's something else 
that connects these two.

00:01:05.648 --> 00:01:07.067
When Xi was just a young boy...

00:01:07.358 --> 00:01:09.027
Mao ruined his life.

00:01:09.819 --> 00:01:12.614
Generations apart, their paths 
crossed unexpectedly

00:01:12.614 --> 00:01:16.159
and a teenage Xi from an 
elite family in Beijing...

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.619
ended up in exile.

00:01:17.619 --> 00:01:19.662
Condemned to hard labor in the countryside.

00:01:20.789 --> 00:01:21.748
50 years later

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:24.793
Xi is one of the most powerful 
political figures in the world

00:01:24.793 --> 00:01:26.753
and the only leader since Mao

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:28.671
to have unchecked power over China.

00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:32.175
So how did he go from 
being banished in his country...

00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:34.636
to taking complete control of it?

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:38.014
[sinister, electronic music]

00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:41.351
[music fades]

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:43.895
Xi Jinping's connection to Mao formed

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:45.772
long before Xi was even born.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.233
[dark, pensive music]

00:01:48.233 --> 00:01:51.402
It goes back to when a bloody civil war 
was raging in China.

00:01:52.654 --> 00:01:56.324
A group of radical communist 
revolutionaries, including Mao

00:01:56.324 --> 00:01:59.160
gained influence over large swaths 
of mainland China...

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:02.664
and controlled a communist military 
called the Red Army...

00:02:02.914 --> 00:02:04.582
that fought the Nationalist Party

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:06.584
ruling the Republic of China at the time.

00:02:07.418 --> 00:02:10.046
At this point, the Communists 
were losing bad.

00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:12.340
The bulk of their army was pinned down here...

00:02:12.507 --> 00:02:15.802
in a communist controlled region
originally established by Mao...

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:18.304
now surrounded by Nationalist forces.

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:20.265
And they were running low on food.

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:23.643
So the Red Army decided to 
launch a bold attack...

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:27.313
to break through the Nationalist forces
and evacuate the roughly

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:30.859
130,000 communist soldiers 
and civilians stuck here.

00:02:32.026 --> 00:02:34.863
On October 16th, 1934, 
they made their move...

00:02:35.405 --> 00:02:37.782
and attacked a weaker part 
of the enemy line.

00:02:38.408 --> 00:02:39.409
They broke through.

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:41.703
And even though their numbers 
quickly dropped

00:02:41.703 --> 00:02:43.997
with thousands dying and 
thousands more

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:45.248
fleeing to the countryside...

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:48.459
around 86,000 stuck together and pushed on.

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.171
This was the beginning of a year-long

00:02:51.171 --> 00:02:53.506
historic retreat called the Long March.

00:02:54.215 --> 00:02:56.467
The journey to establish a new communist base...

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.720
far from the Nationalist forces.

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.808
Mao, who used to be a military leader

00:03:03.808 --> 00:03:05.268
wasn't in charge at this time.

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:07.687
He'd insisted on using 
guerrilla tactics

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:10.356
which had heavily influenced 
the Red Army earlier in the war.

00:03:10.565 --> 00:03:13.443
But that approach had fallen out of favor
and he was demoted.

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:15.278
The Long March changed that.

00:03:16.237 --> 00:03:17.906
After escaping the siege here

00:03:17.906 --> 00:03:20.074
the Red Army continued to suffer
relentless attacks

00:03:20.074 --> 00:03:21.826
by the pursuing Nationalist army.

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:23.494
The military leaders of the march

00:03:23.494 --> 00:03:25.955
had pushed for a more traditional 
wartime strategy

00:03:25.955 --> 00:03:27.415
of direct confrontation...

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:29.417
rather than Mao's guerrilla tactics.

00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:32.170
And the result was catastrophic 
for the Red Army.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.172
Less than half of the original 
escape group

00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:36.090
survived the first three months alone.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:38.551
So it was at this first stopping point

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.511
where Communist Party leader 
Zhou Enlai...

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:42.847
handed military leadership back to Mao.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.643
And Mao picked an end point 
for the march... here.

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:49.812
800 miles away in rural northern China.

00:03:50.438 --> 00:03:51.940
But they didn't go straight for it.

00:03:52.273 --> 00:03:54.442
Mao led the Red Army 
deep into the mountains

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:56.319
where he predicted 
lighter resistance.

00:03:57.111 --> 00:03:58.321
And he was right.

00:03:58.655 --> 00:04:00.448
But the journey was still brutal.

00:04:01.032 --> 00:04:03.451
It was nine more months 
of nonstop marching

00:04:03.451 --> 00:04:06.037
and fighting along this 
several thousand mile route...

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.706
before they ultimately arrived 
in northern China...

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:12.210
where a guerrilla base led by 
a communist revolutaionary

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:15.088
named Xi Zhongxun, offered
Mao's army refuge...

00:04:15.964 --> 00:04:17.465
bringing the Long March 
to an end.

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:21.177
That man was Xi Jinping's father.

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:27.725
In the end, fewer than 8,000 
of the original marchers survived.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.312
Even though thousands died 
on the Long March

00:04:31.312 --> 00:04:33.898
from starvation and fighting and disease...

00:04:34.274 --> 00:04:35.608
Mao's leadership was credited

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:38.194
with saving the Red Army
from total annihilation.

00:04:38.695 --> 00:04:40.780
And he became the de facto 
head of the party

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:42.073
as well as the military...

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:43.866
entrusted with rebuilding the army

00:04:43.866 --> 00:04:47.203
to take on the Nationalist forces 
for total control of China.

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:49.706
“Wherever and however...

00:04:50.081 --> 00:04:51.749
the Red Troops move into battle...

00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:54.127
they spread the glory of Mao Zedong.”

00:04:56.170 --> 00:04:58.506
This is a good stopping point 
in the story to talk about how

00:04:58.506 --> 00:05:01.968
power in the Chinese Communist Party 
or CCP works.

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:04.512
Officially, the highest level of authority

00:05:04.512 --> 00:05:06.764
is a group called the Central Committee

00:05:06.764 --> 00:05:09.892
and is responsible for all of the party's 
major policy decisions.

00:05:10.601 --> 00:05:13.688
Within the Central Committee 
is a select group of officials

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:16.065
called the Political Bureau or Politburo.

00:05:16.607 --> 00:05:19.902
In most Communist parties,
like that of the former Soviet Union...

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:23.323
the Politburo represents the most powerful
members of the party

00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:24.615
besides the General Secretary.

00:05:25.074 --> 00:05:27.493
But the Chinese Communist Party 
has a key distinction

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:28.703
that makes it unique.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:31.456
It has one more 
even smaller selection

00:05:31.456 --> 00:05:33.958
of top officials who ultimately 
have the final say.

00:05:34.292 --> 00:05:38.379
An elite class of Politburo members
called the Politburo Standing Committee.

00:05:38.588 --> 00:05:41.007
This group, which includes the General Secretary

00:05:41.007 --> 00:05:43.801
holds supreme control over 
the Central Committee

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:46.888
dictates the will of the party 
and is in full control

00:05:46.888 --> 00:05:48.431
of the Central Military Commission

00:05:48.431 --> 00:05:50.141
which oversees China's defense.

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:54.145
Mao's promotion during the Long March 
landed him here

00:05:54.145 --> 00:05:56.064
in the highest position of the military 

00:05:56.064 --> 00:05:58.107
and the Politburo Standing Committee.

00:05:58.358 --> 00:06:00.860
Even though he was considered 
the de facto head of the party

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:03.071
when the Long March ended in 1935...

00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:06.115
he officially became head of the party in 1943.

00:06:07.033 --> 00:06:09.660
With both the party and the military 
under his control...

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:12.705
Mao began to exploit the system to ensure

00:06:12.705 --> 00:06:15.124
he remained at the top for the rest of his life.

00:06:17.293 --> 00:06:18.795
At Mao's first National Congress

00:06:18.795 --> 00:06:21.422
as the official party leader in 1945...

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:24.425
the party introduced a resolution 
that brought his influence

00:06:24.425 --> 00:06:25.635
to a whole new level.

00:06:27.095 --> 00:06:30.431
They unified the party around a single 
understanding of its history...

00:06:30.598 --> 00:06:33.017
and declared Mao's political ideology

00:06:33.017 --> 00:06:35.311
later called Mao Tse-Tung Thought 
or Maoism...

00:06:35.645 --> 00:06:37.730
as the unquestioned guiding principle

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:38.981
of the Chinese Communist Party.

00:06:39.941 --> 00:06:42.568
Basically, Mao's ideas or policy decisions

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.570
could no longer be challenged by anyone.

00:06:45.655 --> 00:06:47.990
It was here he unveiled his equivalent
at the time

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:49.492
of the Politburo Standing Committee.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.619
The four other top party leaders...

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:54.455
all long marchers deeply loyal to Mao.

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:56.916
Like Liu Shaoqi and Zhou Enlai...

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.585
who would go on to hold some of 
the most powerful positions

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:00.795
in the future government.

00:07:01.379 --> 00:07:04.215
Four years later, Mao declared
final victory for the Communists

00:07:04.215 --> 00:07:06.050
in a decades long Chinese civil war

00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:07.635
and established a new country:

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.513
The People's Republic of China, 
or PRC.

00:07:11.055 --> 00:07:14.225
“Mao Tse-tung, once a lowly party worker...

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:17.061
now assumed the stature of a dominant figure

00:07:17.061 --> 00:07:18.354
in all of Eastern Asia.”

00:07:20.773 --> 00:07:23.818
But winning a revolution isn't the same 
as running a country.

00:07:24.193 --> 00:07:25.903
Because now that you're running a country

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:27.697
there's all these other things you have to do

00:07:27.697 --> 00:07:31.617
like deliver the mail and like build a dam 
and stuff like that.

00:07:31.659 --> 00:07:34.620
You can't possibly have the party 
do all these things.

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:37.248
So the party set up a government that would take

00:07:37.248 --> 00:07:40.209
the policy decisions made by the 
Politburo Standing Committee

00:07:40.209 --> 00:07:42.378
and figure out a way to make them a reality.

00:07:42.503 --> 00:07:44.964
And so it evolved to a system where...

00:07:45.089 --> 00:07:48.384
the party would make all 
the important decisions...

00:07:48.676 --> 00:07:52.138
and especially the Politburo Standing Committee.

00:07:52.555 --> 00:07:54.140
Then, as it is today...

00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:57.310
many of these decisions would go to 
the State Council.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.938
So the State Council was the highest 
decision-making body

00:08:00.938 --> 00:08:04.525
on the state side, led by 
the Premier of China.

00:08:04.775 --> 00:08:07.570
The Premier of China, by the way, 
is almost always

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.447
a member of the Politburo Standing Committee.

00:08:10.072 --> 00:08:13.075
One way Mao kept a tight grip on power 
over the years

00:08:13.075 --> 00:08:15.912
was by promoting those loyal to him 
to top positions

00:08:15.912 --> 00:08:17.079
in the party in government.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:19.415
Whether they had government experience or not.

00:08:20.416 --> 00:08:21.792
For example, Zhou Enlai...

00:08:22.043 --> 00:08:23.127
the former party leader

00:08:23.127 --> 00:08:25.505
who helped Mao rise to power 
during the Long March...

00:08:26.255 --> 00:08:27.632
became China's first premier.

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:31.552
The advantage of that is that 
they could never challenge him.

00:08:31.928 --> 00:08:33.095
The disadvantage of that is

00:08:33.095 --> 00:08:34.430
they didn't know what they were doing

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.683
and so administration suffered...

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:39.101
policy outcomes suffered.

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:44.232
Long March survivors often became 
party elites under Mao.

00:08:44.774 --> 00:08:47.068
Xi Jinping's father, for example, 
was appointed

00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:48.986
Secretary General of the State Council...

00:08:49.487 --> 00:08:51.656
and as the son of a Long Marcher

00:08:51.656 --> 00:08:54.534
young Xi was given the informal title 
of Princeling.

00:08:55.326 --> 00:08:58.329
Mao succeeded in never giving up 
his power during his lifetime.

00:08:58.829 --> 00:09:01.541
His unchecked policies resulted 
in massive famines

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:03.376
and widespread persecution 

00:09:03.376 --> 00:09:06.921
that cost between 40 and 80 million lives 
over a span of decades...

00:09:07.296 --> 00:09:10.049
and culminated in the disastrous 
Cultural Revolution.

00:09:10.258 --> 00:09:13.302
A violent final attempt from Mao 
to consolidate his power

00:09:13.302 --> 00:09:15.846
and force loyalty to the practice of Maoism.

00:09:17.098 --> 00:09:20.393
The idea was to make himself 
and his ideas eternal.

00:09:21.102 --> 00:09:22.687
The way he viewed communist figureheads

00:09:22.687 --> 00:09:24.397
like Lenin and Marx before him.

00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:27.441
Anyone that didn't fall in line with
Mao’s ideology

00:09:27.441 --> 00:09:28.901
was publicly humiliated...

00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:30.278
impoverished...

00:09:30.278 --> 00:09:32.947
excluded from society, and, 
in many cases

00:09:32.947 --> 00:09:33.614
executed.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.075
Either at the hands of the army...

00:09:36.367 --> 00:09:39.787
or by a militant youth group obsessed 
with enforcing Maoism:

00:09:39.996 --> 00:09:41.205
The Red Guards.

00:09:41.497 --> 00:09:44.458
[cheering]

00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:53.843
Even those closest to the dictator

00:09:53.843 --> 00:09:56.596
weren’t safe from his purges during 
the Cultural Revolution.

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:58.472
High level officials

00:09:58.472 --> 00:10:00.641
including members of the 
Politburo Standing Committee

00:10:00.641 --> 00:10:01.601
and Long Marchers...

00:10:01.726 --> 00:10:03.269
were removed from their positions.

00:10:03.769 --> 00:10:06.397
Liu Shaoqi was denounced 
as a traitor and died

00:10:06.397 --> 00:10:08.399
while imprisoned under harsh conditions.

00:10:08.858 --> 00:10:11.277
Mao also removed the Chief of Staff of the Army

00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:13.070
and replaced him with a Maoist...

00:10:13.237 --> 00:10:15.364
leaving no one left to oppose him 
in the military.

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.199
Mao's handpicked successor

00:10:17.199 --> 00:10:19.118
who always appeared loyally by his side

00:10:19.118 --> 00:10:21.537
in photos and propaganda posters

00:10:21.537 --> 00:10:23.497
died under mysterious circumstances

00:10:23.497 --> 00:10:26.709
when his plane crashed as he was 
fleeing to the Soviet Union.

00:10:27.043 --> 00:10:28.628
Mao later denounced him as a traitor.

00:10:29.962 --> 00:10:31.422
He denounced Xi's father, too.

00:10:31.714 --> 00:10:33.132
This is a photo of Xi senior

00:10:33.132 --> 00:10:35.134
being restrained and publicly criticized

00:10:35.134 --> 00:10:37.845
by the Red Guards at the height 
of the Cultural Revolution.

00:10:38.387 --> 00:10:41.891
He would remain a prisoner in Beijing 
for 8 years following this.

00:10:43.225 --> 00:10:44.435
With his father purged...

00:10:44.518 --> 00:10:46.646
Xi Jinping, 15 at the time

00:10:46.646 --> 00:10:48.689
was expelled from his 
elite school in Beijing

00:10:48.689 --> 00:10:50.316
and sent to work in the countryside.

00:10:51.025 --> 00:10:54.987
He had to live in a cave and
do hard manual labor.

00:10:55.029 --> 00:10:58.032
His food was barely enough for

00:10:58.032 --> 00:11:00.326
a growing young person.

00:11:01.243 --> 00:11:04.080
And Chairman Mao was responsible for all of it.

00:11:05.539 --> 00:11:07.416
But then... Mao died.

00:11:08.042 --> 00:11:09.460
The Cultural Revolution ended.

00:11:10.252 --> 00:11:12.129
His successor was Deng Xiaoping...

00:11:12.463 --> 00:11:14.340
one of the Politburo Standing 
Committee members

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:16.801
who was removed from power 
during the Cultural Revolution.

00:11:17.218 --> 00:11:19.011
Who just before Mao died...

00:11:19.261 --> 00:11:22.390
started making a promise 
to fellow exiled party veterans.

00:11:23.349 --> 00:11:25.601
Deng Xiaoping signaled credibly...

00:11:26.018 --> 00:11:28.270
to all the surviving Long Marchers

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.940
that he wanted to rehabilitate people.

00:11:31.232 --> 00:11:34.777
So when Mao died, they all supported 
the rehabilitation of Deng.

00:11:34.777 --> 00:11:36.696
And as soon as Deng was rehabilitated

00:11:36.696 --> 00:11:39.615
he went ahead and rehabilitated 
all these people.

00:11:39.907 --> 00:11:41.867
With experienced leadership back in place

00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:43.077
the party needed to figure out

00:11:43.077 --> 00:11:44.495
how to prevent something like this...

00:11:49.625 --> 00:11:50.960
from ever happening again.

00:11:51.669 --> 00:11:54.130
In order to undo Mao's 
cult of personality...

00:11:54.338 --> 00:11:57.633
the party introduced a second 
historical resolution in 1981.

00:11:58.217 --> 00:12:00.177
It condemned periods of Mao's rule...

00:12:00.761 --> 00:12:03.723
and emphasized a renewed commitment 
to collective leadership...

00:12:04.265 --> 00:12:06.434
vowing to oppose the consolidation of power

00:12:06.434 --> 00:12:08.227
around one person moving forward.

00:12:08.894 --> 00:12:12.440
The successors to Mao 
didn't want a dictatorship.

00:12:12.481 --> 00:12:15.151
So they divided up these positions

00:12:15.151 --> 00:12:17.737
and put them in the hands of different people.

00:12:18.195 --> 00:12:20.156
Like Mao, Deng kept tight control

00:12:20.156 --> 00:12:22.616
over the Central Military Commission
as its chairman...

00:12:23.033 --> 00:12:25.244
and held a leadership position 
in the government.

00:12:25.244 --> 00:12:26.620
But was never head of state.

00:12:27.163 --> 00:12:29.415
He never held the highest position in the party either.

00:12:29.749 --> 00:12:32.084
Instead, he set up a new advisory commission

00:12:32.084 --> 00:12:33.586
and served as its chairman...

00:12:33.836 --> 00:12:36.046
which allowed him to influence 
the party's direction...

00:12:36.172 --> 00:12:38.466
without positioning himself 
directly on top of it.

00:12:39.008 --> 00:12:40.926
This allowed a power sharing structure

00:12:40.926 --> 00:12:43.471
while still making Deng the 
de facto leader of China

00:12:43.471 --> 00:12:45.306
until he stepped down in 1989...

00:12:45.639 --> 00:12:48.976
following the Army's massacre of students 
protesting in Tiananmen Square.

00:12:49.518 --> 00:12:53.105
“A protester suddenly ran into 
the middle of the street

00:12:53.105 --> 00:12:55.274
and in front of the oncoming tanks.”

00:12:55.483 --> 00:12:58.527
“Anger at Deng Xiaoping, the entire 
Chinese government...

00:12:58.694 --> 00:13:02.740
it had the real feeling of rebellion
in the streets of Beijing.”

00:13:03.115 --> 00:13:06.035
“People want to fight the military 
out of their city.”

00:13:08.454 --> 00:13:09.705
Deng and his ideology

00:13:09.705 --> 00:13:13.334
which moved away from Maoism, 
set a precedent of sharing power.

00:13:13.834 --> 00:13:16.212
He opened up China and 
established economic ties

00:13:16.212 --> 00:13:17.379
with these countries...

00:13:17.505 --> 00:13:21.133
and was the first PRC leader 
to visit the US in 1979.

00:13:21.509 --> 00:13:24.345
“Today, we take another step in the historic...

00:13:24.637 --> 00:13:26.764
normalization of relations

00:13:26.764 --> 00:13:28.724
which we have begun this year.”

00:13:29.475 --> 00:13:31.185
Deng’s reforms became the foundation

00:13:31.185 --> 00:13:33.312
for decades of economic prosperity

00:13:33.312 --> 00:13:36.524
 that led China to having the world's 
second largest economy over time...

00:13:36.774 --> 00:13:39.485
and being on the verge of becoming 
the world's next superpower.

00:13:40.236 --> 00:13:42.863
“Communism is creating a consumer society.”

00:13:42.863 --> 00:13:46.075
“Also reminds you that the standard of living
in China is going up.”

00:13:46.075 --> 00:13:48.160
“Here... capitalism rules.”

00:13:49.829 --> 00:13:52.540
One thing power sharing did lead to...

00:13:53.123 --> 00:13:56.085
was a lot of policy innovation

00:13:56.085 --> 00:13:59.713
and then some degree of decentralization.

00:13:59.713 --> 00:14:05.261
And both of these things helped 
China's economy enormously.

00:14:05.678 --> 00:14:08.138
Which is why this period of 
economic growth and reform

00:14:08.138 --> 00:14:11.225
stretching over roughly 30 years 
is known as the Deng era.

00:14:11.725 --> 00:14:13.811
Whether he wanted to or not,
Deng kept his word...

00:14:13.936 --> 00:14:17.022
and never tried to consolidate 
absolute power around himself.

00:14:17.439 --> 00:14:19.066
He did end up sharing power.

00:14:19.483 --> 00:14:23.779
And that set the stage for 
power sharing in the party...

00:14:24.071 --> 00:14:27.116
until the rise of Xi Jinping.

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:29.535
So what was Xi doing all this time?

00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:31.453
[contemplative music]

00:14:31.453 --> 00:14:33.247
When Mao died in 1976

00:14:33.247 --> 00:14:36.125
Xi was back in Beijing studying 
communist philosophy...

00:14:37.418 --> 00:14:40.421
even though the Chinese Communist Party 
had ruined Xi's family...

00:14:40.504 --> 00:14:41.589
He had joined it.

00:14:41.589 --> 00:14:43.841
Just as the Cultural Revolution 
was winding down.

00:14:44.675 --> 00:14:45.843
But why?

00:14:45.843 --> 00:14:48.137
Personally, he might begrudge 
Chairman Mao

00:14:48.137 --> 00:14:50.556
for doing all this terrible things to his family...

00:14:51.015 --> 00:14:55.352
but I think at this time he also 
recognized that in the system

00:14:55.352 --> 00:14:58.814
of the Chinese Communist Party 
power is everything.

00:14:59.106 --> 00:15:00.649
Without power, you're nothing.

00:15:01.483 --> 00:15:03.027
But in order to get that power...

00:15:03.068 --> 00:15:04.862
Xi did something unexpected.

00:15:05.446 --> 00:15:06.572
He left Beijing.

00:15:06.989 --> 00:15:09.783
As his competitors
were fiercely competing

00:15:09.783 --> 00:15:12.161
with each other in the 
1980s and 1990s.

00:15:12.494 --> 00:15:14.371
He sort of got out of their way...

00:15:14.622 --> 00:15:16.123
and went to the provinces.

00:15:17.124 --> 00:15:18.751
He took positions in party leadership

00:15:18.751 --> 00:15:21.253
in rural, poor provinces 
all around China...

00:15:21.837 --> 00:15:24.173
where there were no other 
princelings to compete with.

00:15:24.632 --> 00:15:28.177
First, in Hebei, a poor rural province 
outside of Beijing...

00:15:28.385 --> 00:15:31.013
where he easily reached the top spot 
as party secretary.

00:15:31.388 --> 00:15:34.016
Then in Fujian, a heavily militarized region

00:15:34.016 --> 00:15:35.976
where top members of the army
were stationed.

00:15:36.352 --> 00:15:38.646
He moved up the ranks 
to party secretary here, too.

00:15:38.771 --> 00:15:41.398
Before becoming the governor of the province 
a few years later.

00:15:42.107 --> 00:15:44.234
After making powerful friends in the military...

00:15:44.276 --> 00:15:46.779
Xi went here... where he once again

00:15:46.779 --> 00:15:48.614
 assumes the office of party secretary...

00:15:48.781 --> 00:15:50.699
and grew his support on the civilian side.

00:15:52.034 --> 00:15:53.494
This strategy of moving around

00:15:53.494 --> 00:15:55.621
didn't just give Xi 
a leg up in areas

00:15:55.621 --> 00:15:56.956
where competition was slim.

00:15:57.581 --> 00:15:58.874
It also gave him credibility...

00:15:59.124 --> 00:16:01.543
as a humble, hard working 
party leader...

00:16:01.961 --> 00:16:04.129
and he cultivated a growing group of supporters

00:16:04.129 --> 00:16:06.423
who would come back into play 
years down the line.

00:16:08.509 --> 00:16:11.053
It was his last stop and his shortest one

00:16:11.053 --> 00:16:12.972
that ultimately got him back to Beijing.

00:16:14.181 --> 00:16:17.601
A brief stint in the top party spot 
in Shanghai in 2007...

00:16:17.768 --> 00:16:19.603
where he rehabilitated the city's image

00:16:19.603 --> 00:16:21.563
following a high-level corruption scandal.

00:16:22.356 --> 00:16:23.816
Xi developed a reputation here

00:16:23.816 --> 00:16:26.318
as a prudent leader who toed the party line.

00:16:26.819 --> 00:16:29.530
Just 7 months later, he finally 
returned to Beijing...

00:16:29.989 --> 00:16:33.575
having been promoted to the all-powerful 
Politburo Standing Committee.

00:16:33.784 --> 00:16:38.706
Basically, the people who were deciding
on top leadership at the time

00:16:38.706 --> 00:16:39.999
they wanted a Princeling.

00:16:39.999 --> 00:16:41.208
But they didn't want a princeling

00:16:41.208 --> 00:16:43.210
who was too ambitious or too strong.

00:16:44.169 --> 00:16:45.754
So Xi Jinping

00:16:45.754 --> 00:16:48.465
he was seen as less ambitious

00:16:48.465 --> 00:16:50.801
because he was willing to go 
to the countryside

00:16:50.801 --> 00:16:53.137
and work in lower-level positions.

00:16:54.013 --> 00:16:55.597
When Xi emerged as the 2007

00:16:55.597 --> 00:16:57.641
National Congress and leadership unveiling...

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:00.477
he was one of the 9 most powerful men 
in the country.

00:17:01.061 --> 00:17:03.522
It was at this moment that his 
strategic climb...

00:17:03.731 --> 00:17:07.151
over 17 long years in the countryside paid off.

00:17:07.568 --> 00:17:08.777
Big time.

00:17:09.695 --> 00:17:11.822
When the General Secretary 
stepped down in 2012...

00:17:12.031 --> 00:17:15.034
Xi emerged at the top spot 
in the party as China's leader.

00:17:15.909 --> 00:17:19.246
Now the elite son of a former revolutionary 

00:17:19.246 --> 00:17:20.456
turned exiled peasant...

00:17:20.914 --> 00:17:22.374
turned party darling...

00:17:22.666 --> 00:17:25.335
was poised to seize control of everything.

00:17:28.839 --> 00:17:31.425
Like Mao, Xi Jinping believes 
that rallying around

00:17:31.425 --> 00:17:33.969
a single figure is crucial 
to the party's survival.

00:17:34.344 --> 00:17:36.013
Rather than the collective leadership

00:17:36.013 --> 00:17:37.598
Deng’s reforms had normalized.

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:40.642
So pretty much as soon as 
Xi Jinping came to power.

00:17:40.768 --> 00:17:42.144
He started getting rid of people.

00:17:43.979 --> 00:17:48.150
“The news... four top officials
removed for taking bribes

00:17:48.150 --> 00:17:49.943
was announced on state TV.”

00:17:49.985 --> 00:17:52.279
“Xi Jinping has just sacked his foreign minister.”

00:17:52.529 --> 00:17:54.406
“Just sacked his defense minister.”

00:17:54.406 --> 00:17:56.325
“He sacked a whole lot of 
other people at the top

00:17:56.325 --> 00:17:57.910
of the military establishment.”

00:17:58.035 --> 00:17:59.203
“The former security czar

00:17:59.203 --> 00:18:01.622
has not been seen in public
for more than a year.”

00:18:01.914 --> 00:18:04.833
“The nvestigation against Zhou 
allows the Chinese president

00:18:04.833 --> 00:18:06.668
to remove those opposed to his reforms.”

00:18:07.002 --> 00:18:10.047
He launched a major anti-corruption campaign...

00:18:10.547 --> 00:18:14.802
as soon as he took power
in late 2012, early ‘13...

00:18:15.344 --> 00:18:19.890
which led to the arrests of
hundreds of senior-level officials

00:18:19.890 --> 00:18:22.267
as well as military officers.

00:18:22.684 --> 00:18:25.229
These purges targeted
Xi's rivals in the party...

00:18:25.312 --> 00:18:28.190
whose vacant positions he filled 
with his own supporters.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:30.150
After this massive purge...

00:18:30.359 --> 00:18:35.322
Xi Jinping was in very tight control over

00:18:35.322 --> 00:18:39.034
both the party and also the Chinese military...

00:18:39.576 --> 00:18:43.122
thereby making him the most powerful leader

00:18:43.122 --> 00:18:45.958
of the Chinese Communist Party 
since the death of Mao.

00:18:46.416 --> 00:18:48.460
That pattern continued in the second term

00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:51.713
where he unveiled 5 new faces 
of the Politburo Standing Committee...

00:18:52.131 --> 00:18:54.883
three of them with close 
personal ties to Xi.

00:18:55.425 --> 00:18:58.971
In 2021, Xi pulled off one of his 
most dramatic acts yet...

00:19:00.180 --> 00:19:02.933
to enforce his influence over 
the Chinese Communist Party.

00:19:04.268 --> 00:19:06.728
He introduced a third historic resolution

00:19:06.728 --> 00:19:08.438
that unified the party's ideology

00:19:08.438 --> 00:19:10.274
around one clear line of thinking:

00:19:10.899 --> 00:19:12.109
Xi Jinping Thought.

00:19:13.193 --> 00:19:15.279
Xi's personal political ideology...

00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:17.865
would now be the core 
in the party's thinking...

00:19:17.990 --> 00:19:19.908
political stance and action.

00:19:20.742 --> 00:19:23.954
Basically, Xi Jinping's ideas 
could no longer be challenged.

00:19:24.538 --> 00:19:26.081
And they weren't for many years.

00:19:27.791 --> 00:19:29.585
During Xi's first 10 years in power

00:19:29.585 --> 00:19:31.670
the size of China's economy 
more than doubled.

00:19:32.588 --> 00:19:34.214
So did average individual income.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:36.425
So did military spending.

00:19:36.758 --> 00:19:37.634
Under his leadership...

00:19:37.801 --> 00:19:40.179
China's presence on the world stage
has grown too. 

00:19:40.179 --> 00:19:41.722
Positioning the rising superpower

00:19:41.722 --> 00:19:44.057
to take on the role of an aggressor, externally.

00:19:44.391 --> 00:19:47.186
Reasserting claims over parts 
of the South China Sea

00:19:47.186 --> 00:19:48.770
Intimidating Taiwan and Tibet

00:19:48.770 --> 00:19:50.898
and stripping democratic process 
in Hong Kong.

00:19:51.940 --> 00:19:53.192
Inside its own borders

00:19:53.192 --> 00:19:55.986
strict Internet censorship and 
surveillance are widespread...

00:19:56.653 --> 00:19:58.238
and oppression of Uyghurs...

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:00.574
a mostly minority Muslim ethnic group...

00:20:00.866 --> 00:20:02.701
is marked by human rights abuses.

00:20:03.619 --> 00:20:05.454
But it wasn't until Covid that Xi saw

00:20:05.454 --> 00:20:07.456
the first real challenge to his authority.

00:20:07.664 --> 00:20:09.583
“Anger in China is growing.”

00:20:10.125 --> 00:20:12.252
“Video showing protester is in Xinjiang...

00:20:12.461 --> 00:20:14.922
fed up with China's zero Covid rules.”

00:20:17.007 --> 00:20:20.052
“The boldest public challenge yet
for leader Xi Jinping.”

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:23.263
With these protests all around the country...

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:25.474
and the party's reputation in peril.

00:20:26.058 --> 00:20:27.643
Xi reversed the failed policy.

00:20:28.810 --> 00:20:31.146
But is now faced with 
a shaky economy...

00:20:31.438 --> 00:20:33.815
and cracks in his unchecked authority.

00:20:39.071 --> 00:20:41.657
It was just weeks before 
the widespread protests

00:20:41.657 --> 00:20:44.201
at the 20th National Congress in 2022...

00:20:45.369 --> 00:20:48.038
that Xi walked out in the leading position
a third time.

00:20:48.455 --> 00:20:51.291
He had already locked in the presidency
for another five years...

00:20:52.167 --> 00:20:54.378
and unveiled a Politburo Standing Committee

00:20:54.378 --> 00:20:56.505
completely packed with those loyal to him.

00:20:56.922 --> 00:20:59.258
After removing the final senior members of the party

00:20:59.258 --> 00:21:00.884
that had ties to his predecessor.

00:21:01.718 --> 00:21:03.720
Now there is no one left in party

00:21:03.720 --> 00:21:06.723
or military leadership whose ideology 
differs from Xi.

00:21:07.724 --> 00:21:10.519
I don't think anyone can 
push him out at this point.

00:21:11.144 --> 00:21:15.232
I think Xi will be the most 
powerful leader in China...

00:21:16.525 --> 00:21:19.403
as long as he's alive and conscious.

00:21:21.405 --> 00:21:23.198
Thanks so much for watching this episode of Atlas.

00:21:23.198 --> 00:21:25.200
So many teammates worked on this piece.

00:21:25.242 --> 00:21:28.245
A small army of editors, animators, 
and researchers

00:21:28.245 --> 00:21:30.664
helped bring this complicated story into focus.

00:21:30.789 --> 00:21:32.165
I especially like to shout out Rajaa...

00:21:32.291 --> 00:21:33.917
one of the key researchers on this piece...

00:21:34.209 --> 00:21:35.877
who conducted an incredible interview

00:21:35.877 --> 00:21:37.296
with our expert Victor Shi.

00:21:37.296 --> 00:21:39.298
It takes a lot of resources to make these videos

00:21:39.298 --> 00:21:40.966
but we publish our work free to watch here 

00:21:40.966 --> 00:21:43.302
because we think journalism should be 
accessible to everyone.

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:45.137
If you believe in keeping journalism free

00:21:45.137 --> 00:21:47.097
and want to support our continuing work

00:21:47.097 --> 00:21:48.807
go to vox.com/give-now

00:21:48.807 --> 00:21:49.766
to make a contribution.

00:21:50.058 --> 00:21:50.559
Thanks again.

